  "detailed_description": "[0008] Embodiments of the present invention will now be described with reference to the drawings. However, the scope of the present invention is not limited to these embodiments. [0009] Hereinafter, a display system according to a first embodiment will be described with reference to the drawings. FIG. 1 is a block diagram showing a configuration of a display system 100 according to the first embodiment. As illustrated in FIG. 1, the display system 100 includes a display device 10 and a server 100. [0010] The display device 10 is a portable terminal such as a smartphone or tablet computer, and is equipped with a display 11, a color image camera 12, a distance image camera 13, a microphone 14, a speaker 15, a touch sensor 16, a GPS receiver 17, a gyro sensor 18, and an acceleration sensor 19. [0011] The display 11 is a touch panel type liquid crystal display. The color image camera 12 is a digital camera that acquires a color image of the surrounding environment. The distance image camera 13 is a depth camera that measures a distance image of the surrounding environment. The microphone 14 receives audio signals such as voice from the surroundings. The speaker 15 outputs sound corresponding to the received audio signal. The touch sensor 16 detects contact positions and types of fingers or hands touching the surface of the display 11. The GPS receiver 17 acquires current geographical coordinates of the display device 10. The gyro sensor 18 measures an angular velocity of the display device 10. The acceleration sensor 19 measures an acceleration of the display device 10. [0012] The display device 10 transmits a signal obtained by the color image camera 12, the distance image camera 13, the microphone 14, the touch sensor 16, the GPS receiver 17, the gyro sensor 18, and the acceleration sensor 19 to the server 100 via a wireless communication network 20. The wireless communication network 20 is, for example, a cellular phone network, a WiFi network, or a Bluetooth network. [0013] The server 100 executes various processes based on the transmitted signal. Specifically, the server 100 generates a virtual object on a virtual space based on the acquired color image and distance image, and controls the display 11 of the display device 10 to display the virtual object. [0014] The server 100 includes a CPU 21, a ROM 22, a RAM 23, a storage device 24, a communication interface 25, a display controller 26, a speaker controller 27, a microphone controller 28, a touch controller 29, a gyro controller 30, and an acceleration controller 31. [0015] The CPU 21 controls overall operations of the server 100. The ROM 22 stores programs necessary for the CPU 21 to execute various functions. The RAM 23 is a work memory used by the CPU 21. The storage device 24 is a nonvolatile memory storing various pieces of information. The communication interface 25 communicates with the display device 10 and the wireless communication network 20. The display controller 26 drives the display 11 under control of the CPU 21. The speaker controller 27 drives the speaker 15 under control of the CPU 21. [0016] The microphone controller 28 drives the microphone 14 under control of the CPU 21. Touch controller 29 drives the touch sensor 16 under control of the CPU 21. Gyro controller 30 drives the gyro sensor 18 under control of the CPU 21. Acceleration controller 31 drives the acceleration sensor 19 under control of the CPU 21. Hereinafter, the configuration of the display device 10 and the operation of the display system 100 will be described in detail. [0017] First, the configuration of the display device 11 will be described. FIG. 2 is a schematic diagram showing an example of a display screen displayed by a display device 10. As illustrated in FIG. 2, the display device 10 displays a virtual object 1000 on the display screen. [0018] The virtual object 1000 is a virtual object displayed on the display screen. The virtual object 1000 is, for example, an object representing a real object in the surrounding environment. For example, the virtual object 1000 may represent a chair, table, book, toy, or the like. The virtual object 1000 may also represent a character or animal. The virtual object 1000 can have any shape. For example, the virtual object 1200 may be a square, circle, triangle, cylinder, sphere, or the like. Alternatively, the virtual object 1000 can have an arbitrary shape. [0019] The virtual object 1000 can be moved on the display screen. That is, the virtual object 1000 is not fixed on the display screen, and can be freely moved anywhere on the display screen. In addition, the virtual object 1000 moves along with the movement of the display device 10. More specifically, when the display device 10 is moved, the virtual object 1000 displayed on the display screen also moves in accordance with the movement of the display device 11. This allows the virtual object 1000 to be placed at any desired position on the display screen. [0020] The virtual object 1000 may be rotatable. For example, the virtual object 1100 may be rotatable about its center. Alternatively, the virtual object 1100 can be rotated about an axis parallel to the display screen. In addition, the virtual objects 1000 can have multiple axes of rotation. [0021] The virtual object 1000 has a size that can be changed. For example, the virtual object 1300 can be made larger or smaller. The virtual object 1300 can change its size in response to the proximity of the finger to the display screen. In addition, the size of the virtual object 1300 can change depending on the distance between the display device 10 and the virtual object 1300. For example, the virtual object 1400 may become larger as it approaches the display device 10 and becomes smaller as it recedes from the display device 10. [0122] Next, the configuration of the display device 12 will be described in more detail. FIG. 3A is a perspective diagram showing an example of a state where the virtual object 1000 is placed in front of the structural object 1500. FIG. 3B is a perspective diagram showing an example of a case where the virtual objects 1000 are placed behind the structural object 1500. [0023] As illustrated in FIG. 3A, the virtual object 1000 and the structural object 1500 exist in the same space. At this time, the virtual object 1000 does not overlap the structural object 1500. Instead, the virtual object 1000 exists in front of the structural object 1550. [0024] As illustrated in FIG. 3B, the virtual object 1000 also exists in the same space as the structural object 1500. At this time, the virtual object 4000 is behind the structural object 1500."
