  "title": "METHOD FOR PROVIDING MACHINE LEARNING ALGORITHM FOR OBJECT DETECTION AND SYSTEMS",
  "technical_field": "The present invention relates to a computer-implemented method for providing a machine learning algorithm for object detection and to systems.",
  "background_art": "[0001] Embodiments of the present disclosure relate to a computer-implemented method for providing machine learning algorithms for object detection, a computer-implemented method for object detection by machine learning algorithms, a system for providing machine learning algorithms for object detection, and a system for object recognition by machine learning algorithms.
[0002] Machine learning is used increasingly frequently in various applications such as autonomous driving, robotics, medical technology, image processing, speech recognition, natural language processing, fraud prevention, and recommendation systems. This involves the use of artificial intelligence (AI), which enables machines to learn independently without explicit programming. In particular, deep learning methods are used widely in AI applications, whereby the performance of the algorithms can be improved by increasing their complexity. For example, convolutional neural networks (CNN) are used in image processing tasks such as object detection and classification. CNNs consist of a large number of neurons arranged in several layers. The basic principle of CNNs is that they process signals through multiple layers. Each layer performs a specific task on the signal before passing it to the next layer. These layers may be fully connected layers, convolutional layers, pooling layers, normalization layers, activation functions, etc.
[0003] When a CNN is trained, the parameters of each layer are adjusted iteratively until the desired level of accuracy is achieved. During this training phase, the error between the predicted output of the network and the actual output is calculated. The backpropagation algorithm is then used to adjust the parameters of each layer so that the error is minimized. The goal is to find a set of parameters that minimize the error and maximize the accuracy of the predictions made by the model.
[0004] Object detection is the task of identifying instances of visual objects within an image and determining a bounding box around them. It is a fundamental problem in computer vision and plays a key role in many practical applications such as autonomous vehicles, security surveillance, augmented reality, and industrial automation. Traditional approaches to object detection typically involve sliding windows or region proposals over the entire image, followed by classification and regression to obtain the final bounding boxes and class labels. However, these methods tend to be computationally expensive and prone to false positives. Deep learning techniques such as Faster R-CNN, YOLO, SSD, and EfficientDet have revolutionized object detection by leveraging end-to-end learning to jointly detect and classify objects directly from feature maps generated by a convolutional neural network (CNN).
[0005] Convolutional Neural Networks (ConvNets) are powerful tools for image classification and object recognition tasks. They consist of several layers, including convolution, max pooling, and fully connected layers. Convolutional layers perform filtering operations on the input image to extract features, while max pooling layers downsample the feature map to reduce its size and computational cost. The fully connected layers combine the extracted features into a high-level representation suitable for classification. Training ConvNets requires large amounts of labeled data and significant computational resources. Transfer learning is a technique that allows fine-tuning a pre-trained model on new tasks with limited data. Fine-tuning involves freezing some layers of the pre-trained model and retraining only the last few layers on the new task. This approach reduces the amount of data required for training and improves the generalization ability of the model.
[0006] Recurrent Neural Networks (RNNs) are another type of neural network commonly used in sequential data processing tasks such as speech recognition, machine translation, and time series prediction. Unlike traditional feedforward neural networks, RNNs maintain an internal state that captures information about previous inputs. This makes them well-suited for tasks involving temporal dependencies, where the current output depends on both the current input and past states. Long Short-Term Memory (LSTM) is a variant of RNN that addresses the vanishing gradient problem common in RNNs by introducing gates that control the flow of information through the hidden state. LSTMs have been instrumental in achieving state-of-the-art results in various sequential data processing tasks.
[0007] Generative Adversarial Networks (GANs) are a type of generative model that uses two neural networks, a generator and a discriminator, in a zero-sum game framework. The generator creates synthetic samples that mimic real data, while the discriminator tries to distinguish between real and fake samples. The adversarial loss function encourages the generator to produce more realistic samples by penalizing incorrect classifications by the discriminator. GANs have been applied successfully in various domains such as image synthesis, style transfer, and semantic manipulation. Variational Autoencoders (VAEs) are another type of generative model that encodes the input data into a latent space and decodes it back into the original space. VAEs impose a prior distribution on the latent variables and optimize a reconstruction loss that measures how closely the reconstructed sample matches the original sample. VAEs have been used for unsupervised learning, anomaly detection, and domain adaptation.
[0008] Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) is a type of machine learning where the model learns representations by predicting missing parts of the input data or finding relationships among different views of the same data. SSL can leverage large amounts of unlabeled data for pretraining and improve the generalization ability of models. Examples of self-supervised tasks include rotation prediction, jigsaw puzzles, and masked language modeling. SSL has been successful in various applications such as image classification, object detection, and natural language processing.",
